<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zacharias Ursinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1534">1534</a>–<a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zacharias Ursinus was a German Reformed theologian, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> and a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>. Called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1561 under Elector Frederick III, he was the principal author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> of 1563. After the Lutheran Elector Louis VI dismissed the Reformed teachers in 1576, he taught at Neustadt an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haardt">Haardt</a> until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zacharias-ursinus.jpg" alt="Zacharias Ursinus" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zacharias_Ursinus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zacharias Ursinus was born Zacharias Baer on 18 July 1534 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a>, then in Silesia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and now Wrocław in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name came from ursus, bear, after his German surname. At fifteen he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-wittenberg">University of Wittenberg</a> and boarded for seven years with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a>, who admired his gifts and commended him to scholars across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He was also a lifelong protégé of the imperial physician Johannes Crato von Krafftheim, another native of Wrocław.</p><p>He went on to study at Strasbourg, Basel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and studied under Jean Mercier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, a pamphlet he published on the sacraments led Lutherans to charge him with being more Reformed than Lutheran, and their opposition drove him to Zurich, where he became a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a> and Pietro Martire Vermigli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On Vermigli's recommendation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-palatine">Frederick III, Elector Palatine</a>, made him a professor in the Collegium Sapientiae at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1561. Commissioned by the elector, he supplied the preliminary drafts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> and took part in its final revision with other theologians and church leaders. Caspar Olevianus was once held to be a co-author, a view modern scholarship has largely discarded. After Frederick's death and the accession of a Lutheran elector in 1576 Ursinus was removed, and from 1578 he held a chair at a Reformed academy at Neustadt. He died there on 6 May 1583, aged 48.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His works were published in 1587 to 1589, and a fuller edition by his son and two pupils, David Pareus and Quirinius Reuter, appeared in 1612. His collected lectures on the catechism, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> as Het Schatboeck, became one of the most prominent theological handbooks among Reformed Christians of the seventeenth century, above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Reformed German and Dutch immigrants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> honoured his memory, and Ursinus College in Collegeville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, founded in 1869, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zacharias Ursinus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick III created a church council to run the Reformed church and sponsored the Heidelberg Catechism of 1563, chiefly the work of <b>Zacharias Ursinus</b>, and in 1582 the territory received a common law code, the Landrecht.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Neustadt an der Weinstraße, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.11,17.03&amp;year=1565" aria-label="Wrocław on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4937 392 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zacharias Ursinus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 662h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 662h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.11,17.03&amp;year=1565">What was here in 1565? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
